Two constants that encode the pointer's width, and one alignment that does.

MRB_STR_EMBED_LEN_BITS is 5, so an embedded string's length must fit in 31. The
space available is sizeof(void*)*3 + sizeof(void*) - 5, which is 59 once a pointer
is 16 bytes, and the static assertion in string.c fires. Six bits is the smallest
value that fits and costs one bit of the flags word.

mrb_alignas(8) on a `static const struct RProc` asks for less alignment than the
type requires once its members are capabilities, which clang rejects outright.
sizeof(void*) is the honest expression of what the original 8 meant.

Both are no-ops anywhere a pointer is 8 bytes.

diff --git i/include/mruby/string.h w/include/mruby/string.h
index a68b959..f851b4e 100644
--- i/include/mruby/string.h
+++ w/include/mruby/string.h
@@ -66,7 +66,7 @@ struct RStringEmbed {
    to 16: RSTRING_EMBED_LEN_MAX is 11 on 32-bit and 27 on 64-bit, both of which
    five bits hold. */
 #define MRB_STR_EMBED_LEN_SHIFT 4
-#define MRB_STR_EMBED_LEN_BITS 5
+#define MRB_STR_EMBED_LEN_BITS 6
 #define MRB_STR_EMBED_LEN_MASK (((1 << MRB_STR_EMBED_LEN_BITS) - 1) << MRB_STR_EMBED_LEN_SHIFT)
 
